  [
    id 294
    label "WxmWgeQWlOE"
    step 0
    date "2020-06-11T18:30:02Z"
    title "Elvis Presley - Summer 1958. Studio B and Gladys Presley death"
    description "Where was Elvis only recording session during his army duty? Where was Elvis's mother Gladys originally buried? We visit sites in Memphis and Nashville&#10;   TIMESTAMP&#10;0:00 May 1958&#10;0:17 RCA Studio B, Nashville&#10;4:05 Gladys falls ill&#10;4:27 Gladys dies at Methodist Hospital, Memphis&#10;5:21 Site Memphis Funeral Home&#10;6:17 Gladys Presley buried at Forest Hill Cemetery, Memphis&#10;7:55 September 1958&#10;8:07 Outro&#10;&#10;Elvis Presley - Summer 1958. Studio B and Gladys Presley death. The latest in our potted Elvis Presley biography compiled from our 2017 and 2019 holidays that concentrates on locations we have been lucky enough to have visited. At the beginning of the summer of 1958 Elvis Presley was coming to the end of basic training at Fort Hood, Texas. Elvis was then entitled to his 2 week furlough. This was time that saw Elvis home in Memphis with his parents Gladys Presley and Vernon Presley along with friends and other Elvis family members. Business also needed 'taking care of business' and so one night was set aside for Elvis to record new tracks at RCA's new recording studio in Nashville - Studio B. In an all-night session on June 10th Elvis recorded 5 songs, all of which would become Elvis classics - I Need Your Love Tonight, (Now And Then There's) A Fool Such As I, A Big Hunk O' Love, I Got Stung and Ain't That Loving You Baby. Elvis returned to Fort Hood and this time Elvis parents soon followed to live off-base in Killeen. The family reunion would be relatively short-lived as Gladys' health deteriorated and Gladys and Vernon returned home to Memphis. Elvis finally was granted leave to return home but Gladys took a turn for the worse and passed away on 14th August. The funeral of Gladys Presley was on 16 August 1958 and soon after Gladys death, summer 1958 ended as Elvis left for West Germany on USS Randall from Brooklyn.....&#10; Sources of information include books by Peter Guralnick, Ernst Jorgensen, Pal Granlund, David English, Erik Lorentzen, Alan Hanson, Paul Richardson and Keith Flynn. Websites include elvisinfonet.com, elvis.com/au, historic-memphis.com, and the fascinating stories and information contributed by so many to the sites, and magazines Elvis The Man and His Music and Essential Elvis. Also the Memphis locations books Elvis - Memphis Style by Mike Freeman and Follow Me To Memphis by Andrew Hearn.&#10;&#10;Take a look at our other travel content at: @Ocean Hops&#10;&#10;31 May 1958 - Following completion of his basic training at Fort Hood, Texas, Elvis returns home for a two-week furlough.&#10;Elvis goes to Memphis Fairgrounds and the Rainbow Rollerdrome and last Presley family pictures before Gladys' death &#10;Elvis will spend an evening in Nashville for a RCA recording session at their new studio&#10;The most famous studio in Nashville -  RCA Studio B.&#10;&#34;RCA Records established a recording studio in this building November 1957 with the local offices run by guitarist/producer Chet Atkins. Studio B recorded&#160;&#160;numerous hits by Elvis, the Everlys, Roy Orbison, Don Gibson, Charlie Pride, Jim Reeves, Dolly Parton and many others. Along with Bradley Studios, Studio B is known for developing the 'Nashville sound'.&#34; &#10;So this studio was built after the studio that RCA were using on McGavock Street was no longer fit for purpose, that they were sharing with the Methodist Film TV and Radio Commission, &#10;and this one was purpose-built and Chet Atkins was&#160;tasked with actually building it - designing it.&#10;So the first time Elvis recorded here was on June 10 1958.  Elvis was on leave from army and this would be the only time that Elvis officially recorded while he was in the army. June 10 1958 all-night session Elvis recorded five songs I Need Your Love Tonight,  (Now And Then There's) A Fool Such&#160;As I, Ain't That Loving You Baby,  I Got Stung and A Big Hunk O' Love, &#10;So Elvis back to Fort Hood&#160;for his advanced tank training four days later. &#10;Gladys and Vernon Presley follow Elvis to Texas to live off-base in Killeen.&#10;9 August 1958 - On the same day Elvis completes his Advanced Tank Training, Gladys is admitted to Methodist Hospital &#10;12 August 1958 - After Gladys' condition worsens, Elvis is given leave to return to Memphis.&#10;14 August 1958 - After spending the whole day before at Gladys' bedside, Elvis has returned to Graceland. In the early hours he receives a phone call from Vernon that Gladys has died...&#10;So following Gladys' death on August 14 1958&#160;here at the Methodist Hospital&#10;Memphis Funeral Home was right across the road Elvis was back at the funeral home the next day as his friend Red West's father had also died. We have taken a stop at Forest Hill Cemetery again. &#10;this was Gladys's original burial site. This is where the statue that now stands in the Meditation Garden in Graceland once stood.&#10;22 September 1958 - Elvis leaves Brooklyn on USS Randall bound for Bremherhaven docks, West  Germany and ultimately his final destination of Freiburg.&#10;&#10;&#10;&#8203;"
    channeltitle "Ocean Hops"
    channelid "UCTdPXH_j-pIcV9b533hntGw"
